#   patch_sha256            34180795a058a087c057dddf171652c5697350a753a0a1af11c2bb548205c718
#   hunks/added/removed     9 / 359 / 0
#   bytes / lines           5393 -> 23874  /  151 -> 510
#   methods                 __init__ pre_forward _start_load_kv post_forward
#   methods_pre_sha256 / methods_post_sha256: the PRE values are read from the box's
#                           real file by the engine interpreter (3.13) and the POST
#                           values by the identical AST program run by the SAME
#                           interpreter against the applied image piped in as base64 -
#                           so no cross-version drift is being assumed. Both runs
#                           printed `COMPILE ok` and the file digest above, so the
#                           post-image is the one the engine would import and not a
#                           local reconstruction. The build path is the tool's own:
#                           `git apply --check -p1` clean at zero offset on a freshly
#                           scp'd pre-image, then `git apply -p1`, then the result
#                           compared byte-for-byte with the locally built image.
#   methods_pre_sha256      "97e1699f0f70fcda0b8877b283f289b9027a2eb8ea2e2852cc267465766e59da c3c8f1f3dda46488c360a06f12b45df28c5af4920a1bba65ac5679cc99551a0b 6eefbbef6bc4849b071a3d7c6ade76ac82591605605122c9713f2cb28ab973c9 890072097bd4bf6811d30a231a2a9168add3ced60b8b86e39616bcf425feebdd"
#   methods_post_sha256     "a953dc2593831a6e8040b817864bdbcbdf9dab51e2682b03ce65a455ba0cd824 c5d43d2a71c437a4aecce923e02aa1939e62bb5bae08b78d2679628aac8bf8f6 8025b22d91d82181415606e806113f6c263286f17bf191737433385570f00044 6d4a03ccf12620243ca43b7bbea445a56dbdb0339ecac315152bd49f88f5d156"
#   new members (no pre-image by definition, carried by the whole-file hashes):
#                           set_load_collision_sink (on BOTH classes: the interface stub
#                           so the runner may register unconditionally, and the real one),
#                           _kvtier_capture_load_submit, _kvtier_publish_inflight_loads,
#                           _kvtier_retire_completed, _kvtier_push_guard,
#                           _kvtier_guard_witness_path, _kvtier_write_guard_witness,
#                           plus the module-level `logger` and the two KVTIER_ constants.
#                           FINGERPRINTING IS NOT A BLOCKER HERE, unlike DRAFT-0005: every
#                           function this hunk adds is a METHOD of a declared klass, and
#                           the four methods it MODIFIES all already exist, so the
#                           fingerprinter has a pre-image for each. The module-level names
#                           it adds carry no fingerprint and are covered by the whole-file
#                           hashes alone - said rather than glossed, since the tool has no
#                           way to say it.
#
# ---- THE FOURTH FILE: one line, because the runner is the only join ----------
#   module                  vllm.v1.worker.gpu.model_runner
#   klass                   GPUModelRunner
#   git_rel                 vllm/v1/worker/gpu/model_runner.py
#   target_mode             UNKNOWN
#   tree_v2_5_1_target_abs  /home/user/vllm-38-next-v2.5/vllm-v2.5/vllm/v1/worker/gpu/model_runner.py
#   tree_v2_5_1_git_root    /home/user/vllm-38-next-v2.5/vllm-v2.5
#   tree_v2_5_1_engine_python /home/user/vllm-38-next-v2.5/venv-v2.5/bin/python
#   tree_v2_5_1_file_sha_pre  ac31600f478559a98d8179411037873d42632da00dec27c4cae49b69e4000efa
#   tree_v2_5_1_file_sha_post c4b0a60eed46696f1eb2a567e30c635e85cb7273ce6b70d58701a43bcd75a0f1
#   patch                   model_runner.py.patch
#   patch_sha256            562e4116d349fb851bdedf5722ef691beeb090844aee2f2575d493022ad80e13
#   hunks/added/removed     1 / 9 / 0
#   bytes / lines           118566 -> 119233  /  2659 -> 2668
#   methods                 initialize_kv_cache
#   methods_pre_sha256      "36c313eec8f2007bcc6ab43fb292e49dc62515d7b2a745db86f51538ae2894db"
#   methods_post_sha256     "305625149b53a6f38dda253f9f24344c518ac6716877f395ae2d8f02d127f41a"
# Why a setter and not a constructor argument: `get_kv_connector` has exactly one
# caller, but the NO_OP connector is a module-level singleton built at import, so the
# call has to be answered by the interface either way. Registering unconditionally
# (outside the `is_profiling` branch) is the point - a skipped registration would be
# indistinguishable from an un-armed guard, and the interface stub makes the call
# legal on every connector the runner can hold.
#
# ---- THE LOAD SIDE'S COST, MEASURED (same host, engine venv python 3.13) ------
# The only UNCONDITIONAL new per-step work is two host-side calls on a step that has a
# connector and no load (the plugin's dev-door lesson is that work on the step loop
# stalls serving, so these are priced against a bare Python call measured alongside):
#     bare Python call, same loop                    0.016 us
#     capture on a step with no load  (per step)     0.072 us
#     publish with nothing pending/outstanding       0.049 us   (called at the submit)
#     publish, one event and 320 ids outstanding     0.051 us
#     retire of a proven event                       0.079 us
# Steps that ACTUALLY load pay once each, for turning ids into a frozenset: capture
# with 320 destination ids 2.659 us, the full submit cycle 7.300 us. A load step is
# already a step the tier chose to slow down, and the guard's whole purpose is that
# step; the settle allowance this box grants a restore is 5,000 ms.
#
# ---- OBSERVABILITY: "armed" must be provable from outside the process --------
# Every publish, un-publish, expiry and check is counted on the connector, and the
# counters are published twice: once as a WARNING line at the first arming (which the
# proxy already ingests from `/tmp/vllm-serve.log` into `logs.db`, so the dashboard
# shows it with no plugin change), and once as a JSON witness at
# `$VLLM_E2_TIER_DIAG/recoverssm-guard-<PPn_TPk>.json` (`/tmp` when unset - so a
# missing diagnostics directory never makes "no file" mean "no loads"). The file is
# SAMPLED, not an event log: a rewrite happens when the outstanding count moves or
# after `KVTIER_GUARD_WITNESS_EVERY` (64) accumulated changes, because this code runs
# on the step loop. Its `verdict` key is the answer to "do not let a zero look like a
# pass": the four states in "WHAT IS MISSING" differ in nothing else, and the file
# carries its own reading rule in a `meaning` key so an operator does not need this
# manifest to interpret it. The name never begins with `rank-`, so the reader that
# walks that directory for rank documents cannot mistake it for one (a test pins it).
# Writes are wrapped in `except (OSError, TypeError, ValueError)`, counted as
# `witness_write_failures`, and the log call inside that handler passes no `exc_info`
# - a diagnostic path that can raise is how a witness silently becomes an assumption.

# ---- what these hunks are NOT tested by ------------------------------------
# `tests/test_recoverssm_load_collision_guard.py` (18 tests) exercises the COLLISION
# PREDICATE in-process, without an engine, by extracting the guard's bytes out of the
# patch file and executing them - so the tests run the shipped code and cannot drift from
# it. It proves: the raise, the message naming row + block + which side was second, that
# it is a real exception type with no statement-level `assert` in the added lines, both
# directions of "seen second", the inert-until-published contract, the guard's position
# ahead of the commit loop, and the fast-path/zero-allocation claims. A mutation that
# swaps the raise for `ValueError` fails 4 of the 18.
# What it CANNOT test: `mamba_hybrid.py`'s resolution hunk. It needs tensors and a real
# `BlockTables`, so it is compile-verified only. The manifest says so rather than letting
# the file count as coverage.
#
# `tests/test_recoverssm_guard_wiring.py` (25 tests) covers the LOAD SIDE the same way:
# the shipped `+` lines of `kv_connector.py.patch` are carved out and exec'd, and the
# sink they publish into is the REAL `RecoverSSMState` the sibling suite extracts, so a
# publish travels through both hunks into the shipped exception in one process. It pins:
# the publish/un-publish round trip (armed, then disarmed, and the commit-side gate
# closing); that an un-retired event arms a LATER step's commit (the async case that a
# per-step clear would have hidden); a load shared by two requests retiring only when
# both are reported; a failed load retiring like a success; a bound that never submitted
# never being published; a stale bind note not leaking into the next submit; the
# expiry-when-unprovable counter; that the collision exception is never swallowed by the
# counter or witness path; that an unwritable witness still serves AND says so; the
# three-way verdict distinction; the witness being sampled rather than per-step; and the
# unarmed path priced against a bare call measured in the same loop. Three of its tests
# are STRUCTURAL (they parse the patch for the publish at the submit site, the capture
# after the bind, the retire against `transfer_results`, and the runner's registration),
# because those four lines sit inside methods no in-process test can call; deleting the
# publish line from the hunk was checked to fail one of them.

